## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of a beginner-friendly CQRS/event-sourcing workflow with cross-service coordination, compensation patterns, and concrete read model projections.

## Key Concepts

- Append-only EventStore per aggregate root
- Simple saga orchestration with compensation logic
- Read model projection from event streams
- Event-driven communication between bounded contexts

## Code Example

```javascript
// Data contracts
class OrderPlaced { constructor(orderId, items, amount) { this.orderId = orderId; this.items = items; this.amount = amount; } }
class InventoryReserved { constructor(orderId) { this.orderId = orderId; } }
class PaymentAuthorized { constructor(orderId) { this.orderId = orderId; } }
class PaymentFailed { constructor(orderId, reason) { this.orderId = orderId; this.reason = reason; } }
class InventoryRejected { constructor(orderId, reason) { this.orderId = orderId; this.reason = reason; } }

// Append-only EventStore per order
class EventStore {
  constructor() { this.events = new Map(); }
  append(orderId, event) {
    if (!this.events.has(orderId)) this.events.set(orderId, []);
    this.events.get(orderId).push(event);
  }
  getEvents(orderId) { return this.events.get(orderId) || []; }
}

// Saga Orchestrator
class OrderSaga {
  constructor(eventStore, inventoryService, paymentService) {
    this.eventStore = eventStore;
    this.inventory = inventoryService;
    this.payment = paymentService;
  }
  async handle(event) {
    if (event instanceof OrderPlaced) {
      this.eventStore.append(event.orderId, event);
      const inventoryResult = await this.inventory.reserve(event.orderId, event.items);
      if (inventoryResult.success) {
        this.eventStore.append(event.orderId, new InventoryReserved(event.orderId));
        const paymentResult = await this.payment.authorize(event.orderId, event.amount);
        if (paymentResult.success) {
          this.eventStore.append(event.orderId, new PaymentAuthorized(event.orderId));
        } else {
          this.eventStore.append(event.orderId, new PaymentFailed(event.orderId, paymentResult.reason));
          this.compensate(event.orderId);
        }
      } else {
        this.eventStore.append(event.orderId, new InventoryRejected(event.orderId, inventoryResult.reason));
      }
    }
  }
  compensate(orderId) {
    const events = this.eventStore.getEvents(orderId);
    events.reverse().forEach(event => {
      if (event instanceof InventoryReserved) {
        this.inventory.release(orderId);
      }
    });
  }
}

// Read Model Projection
class OrderStatusProjection {
  constructor(eventStore) {
    this.eventStore = eventStore;
    this.orderStatus = new Map();
  }
  project(orderId) {
    const events = this.eventStore.getEvents(orderId);
    let status = 'Pending';
    events.forEach(event => {
      if (event instanceof PaymentAuthorized) status = 'Completed';
      else if (event instanceof PaymentFailed || event instanceof InventoryRejected) status = 'Failed';
    });
    this.orderStatus.set(orderId, status);
    return status;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests design of robust, multi-tenant cache invalidation with Redis and Memcached, covering versioning, streams, stampede protection, and testability.

## Key Concepts
- Versioned per-tenant keys
- Redis Streams or Pub/Sub for invalidation
- Stampede protection via short TTLs and mutexes
- Cross-service invalidation consistency
- Observability and test plans

## Code Example
```javascript
function publishInvalidation(tenantId, itemId, ver){
  redis.xadd(`cache_invalidate:tenant:${tenantId}`, '*', 'item', itemId, 'ver', ver);
}
async function readItem(tenantId, itemId){
  const v = await redis.get(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:v`);
  const cached = await redis.get(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:data`);
  if (cached && v === latestVersionFromStore(itemId)){
    return JSON.parse(cached);
  } else {
    const fresh = await fetchFromStore(tenantId, itemId);
    await redis.set(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:data`, JSON.stringify(fresh));
    await redis.set(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:v`, latestVersionFromStore(itemId));
    return fresh;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this adapt to Memcached-only environments?
- How would you measure correctness and latency impact?
- How would you handle cascading invalidations across services?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical cache invalidation strategies in multi-region Redis deployments, testing understanding of versioned cache keys, cross-region synchronization, and TTL management. It also assesses knowledge of Redis pub/sub versus keyspace notifications and write-through caching trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Versioned cache keys with TTL management
- Cross-region invalidation patterns
- Redis pub/sub vs keyspace notifications
- Atomic operations and consistency guarantees
- Write-through vs write-behind caching strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: bumpVersion(id)
const newVer = await redis.incr(`product:${id}:ver`);
await redis.publish('cache:invalidate', JSON.stringify({id, ver: newVer}));
```

```javascript
// Pseudo: getCache(id)
const currentVer = await redis.get(`product:${id}:ver`);
const cached = await redis.get(`product:${id}:v${currentVer}`);
if (!cached) {
  // Cache miss - refresh from source
  const data = await fetchFromDatabase(id);
  await redis.setex(`product:${id}:v${currentVer}`, 120, JSON.stringify(data));
  return data;
}
return JSON.parse(cached);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Understanding how to coordinate multiple services using a Saga with event sourcing and CQRS is core to reliable backend systems.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration across Inventory, Payment, Shipping
- Event-sourcing with an event store per aggregate
- Idempotent handlers and outbox for exactly-once delivery
- Compensating actions for failure paths

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal orchestrator sketch (simplified)
function onEvent(event){
  const cid = event.correlationId;
  const state = loadState(cid) ?? {step:0, orderId: event.orderId};
  switch(state.step){
    case 0:
      publishCommand('ReserveInventory', {orderId: state.orderId, items: event.items, correlationId: cid});
      state.step = 1; saveState(cid, state); break;
    case 1:
      publishCommand('ChargePayment', {orderId: state.orderId, amount: event.amount, correlationId: cid});
      state.step = 2; saveState(cid, state); break;
    case 2:
      publishCommand('ScheduleShipment', {orderId: state.orderId, address: event.address, correlationId: cid});
      state.step = 3; saveState(cid, state); break;
    case 3:
      markComplete(cid); break;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test compensation paths and idempotency?
- How do you ensure exactly-once delivery across Outbox and message buses?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design robust Saga orchestration with cross-region isolation, outbox reliability, and per-tenant event schemas in an environment using CQRS and event-sourcing.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration across regions with compensations
- Outbox pattern for durable cross-service communication
- Event-sourcing with per-tenant schema versioning
- Idempotency via causationId and replayability for recovery

## Code Example
```javascript
class RideSaga {
  constructor(rideId, tenant) {
    this.rideId = rideId;
    this.tenant = tenant;
    this.state = 'RideCreated';
  }
  start() {
    publish({ type: 'RideCreated', rideId: this.rideId, tenant: this.tenant });
    this.next('ReserveDriver');
  }
  next(step) {
    // emit next step event
  }
  onEvent(e) {
    if (e.type === 'PaymentFailed') this.compensate('CollectPayment');
  }
  compensate(failedStep) {
    // issue compensations in reverse order
    publish({ type: 'Compensation', rideId: this.rideId, step: failedStep, tenant: this.tenant });
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How to guarantee exactly-once delivery across regions?
- How to test sagas with replay and failure modes?
- How to evolve event schemas without breaking tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend multiple token types, implement a small, pluggable policy engine, and ensure tenant-scoped access with TTL caching. It also touches SAML adapters and middleware integration.

## Key Concepts
- Token normalization, per-tenant policy language, TTL cache
- SAML-to-claims adapter, middleware enforcement
- Edge cases: clock skew, cache invalidation, tenant isolation

## Code Example
```typescript
// Pseudo-implementation sketch
type Claims = { tenant:string; scopes:string[]; sub:string }
function normalize(token:string):Claims { /* validate OIDC/SAML and map to internal claims */ }
function loadPolicy(tenant:string, path:string):Policy|undefined { /* TTL cache */ }
function authorize(token:string, path:string):boolean {
  const c = normalize(token);
  const p = loadPolicy(c.tenant, path);
  return p ? c.scopes.includes(p.requiredScope) && c.tenant===p.tenant : false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you propagate policy updates across a multi-instance deployment?
- How would you test the policy engine and TTL cache behavior?
- How would you handle token revocation and clock skew in this design?

